{ Plans from Chess Strategy, by Edward Lasker, a leading master of the 1910s. First, the open column: a column with no pawns, where a rook runs all the way to his second row. Paris, 1914, and you have Black, as Lasker did: rook to b8. [%cal Ya8b8] }
1... Rb8 { Three columns are open, and you took the b column before his rook could. From b2 your rook would hit two of his pawns. [%cal Gb8b2][%csl Ra2,Rc2] } (1... Be6 { The bishop to e6 is a good move and level too, but it lets his rook reach the b column first. } 2. Rfb1 { He puts a rook on the b column before taking the pawn on c6. } 2... Rab8 3. Bxc6 { Level: his rook got to the b column first, so yours can only stare at it. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (1... Rd8 { The rook takes the d column instead, and the b column is left for his rook. } 2. Rab1 { His rook jumps onto the empty b column. } 2... Rd6 3. Rfe1 { Level: his rooks hold the b and e columns, and yours went to d6 to guard a pawn. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (1... Re8 { The rook takes the e column, but look at his bishop: it takes on c6 and then hits both your rooks. } 2. Bxc6 Be6 3. Bxe8 Rxe8 { Much better for him: his bishop took a whole rook, and you only got the bishop back. [%cal Ga8b8] }) (1... Bd7 { The bishop guards the pawn from d7. Level, but a bishop minding a pawn does little else, and the b column is left for him. } 2. Rab1 Rab8 3. Rb3 { Level: his rook reached the b column first, and your bishop still has one job, guarding a pawn. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (1... Bb7 { The bishop guards the pawn from b7, but it stands right on the open column, where his rook can hit it. } 2. Rab1 Ba6 3. Rfe1 { Level: your bishop was pushed to a6, and the b column belongs to his rook. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (1... Bf5 { The bishop comes out to f5. Level, but your rooks wait while his rook takes the b column. } 2. Rfb1 { His rook grabs the b column first; the pawn on c6 can wait. } 2... Rab8 3. Rb3 { Level: your rook arrives, but his is already there, so the column is shared, not yours. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (1... a5 { The a pawn goes forward. Level, but it does nothing for your rooks, and his rook takes the b column. } 2. Rfb1 Ra6 3. Rb3 { Level: his rook owns the b column, and yours on a6 is busy guarding the c pawn. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (1... g6 { A breathing hole for your king. Level, but it hands him the free move. } 2. Rfb1 { He uses it: his rook takes the b column, and the pawn on c6 can wait. } 2... Be6 3. Rb7 Bd5 { Level, but now it is his rook on your second row, not yours on his. [%cal Gb8b2] }) 2. Bxc6 { He takes the pawn on c6 to win his pawn back. Now run your rook along the column to his second row: rook to b2. [%cal Yb8b2] } 2... Rb2 { Your rook reaches his second row and hits both pawns next to it, and nothing guards the one on c2. [%csl Ra2,Rc2][%cal Gb2c2] } (2... Rd8 { The other rook takes the d column. Level, but neither rook of yours reaches his second row. } 3. Ba4 c5 4. Bb3 { Level: his bishop now sits on b3, right across the column your rook needed. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (2... Be6 { The bishop first. Level, but it gives him a move to organise his defence before your rook comes. } 3. f4 g6 4. Rf3 Rb2 { Level: the rook gets to his second row all the same, one move later than it could have. [%csl Gb2] }) (2... g6 { A breathing hole first. Level, but his bishop gets back in time to block the column. } 3. Bd5 Rd8 4. Bb3 { Level: his bishop stands on b3, across the b column, and your rook on b8 cannot pass. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (2... Bf5 { The bishop develops to f5 first. Level, and your rook still reaches b2, but a move later. } 3. Rfe1 Rb2 4. Ba4 { Level: your rook got to his second row late, and his rook took the e column meanwhile. [%csl Gb2] }) (2... Rb4 { The rook stops on the fourth row. Level, but his rook now fights you for the b column. } 3. Rab1 Rc4 4. Bd5 { Level: your rook went sideways to c4, and the b column belongs to his rook now. [%cal Gb8b2] }) (2... Rb6 { The rook attacks his bishop, but it stops short of his second row. } 3. Bd5 Be6 4. Bb3 c5 { Level: his bishop settled on b3, and your rook's road along the b column is closed. [%cal Gb6b2] }) 3. Be4 { His bishop hurries back to guard the c pawn. Now your bishop to e6, where it guards d7, his way in on the d column. [%cal Yc8e6,Ge6d7,Re4c2] } 3... Be6 { The d column is open too, but with your bishop on e6 his rooks have no way in there. Now watch your other rook join the first. [%tail][%cal Ge6d7] } (3... Re8 { The rook takes the e column. Level, but your bishop still sits at home, and d7 has no guard. } 4. f3 Be6 5. Rfe1 { Level: the bishop went to e6 anyway, a move later, and his rook now meets yours on the e column. [%cal Gc8e6] }) (3... Bd7 { The bishop stands on d7 itself, but there his rook can hit it at once. } 4. Rfd1 Be6 { Level: his rook chased your bishop to e6 anyway, and now his rook sits on the d column for free. [%cal Ge6d7] }) (3... Rb5 { The rook steps back to b5. Level, but it leaves his second row, and his rook takes the b column behind it. } 4. Rfb1 Rc5 5. a4 { Level: your rook is off his second row, and his rook holds the b column. [%cal Gb5b2] }) (3... Rb4 { The rook attacks his bishop from b4, but it gives up his second row. } 4. f3 Be6 5. a3 { Level: his pawn chased your rook, and nothing of yours stands on his second row now. [%csl Gb2] }) (3... Bg4 { The bishop comes out to g4, where it hits nothing, and d7 stays without a guard. } 4. Rfe1 Rd8 5. h4 Be6 { Level: the bishop comes back to e6 in the end, and his rook took the e column meanwhile. [%cal Gc8e6] }) 4. f4 Rfb8 { Both your rooks stand on the b column, one on his second row, hitting a2 and c2, and his rook and bishop are stuck guarding them. That is the open column. [%csl Ra2,Rc2][%cal Gf8b8] } 0-1


