{ Wilna, 1912: you have White against Akiba Rubinstein, one of the best players of his day. The d column is open, and your rook stands on it. }
17... Kf7 { His king steps to f7, a mistake: the pawn to g6 was better. The open column again: can you get a rook onto his second row? } 18. Bd7 { Your bishop lands on d7 and hits his rook and his bishop at once. His best is to take, and your rook takes back with check. [%cal Gd7c8,Gd7f5] } (18. h4 { The h pawn goes forward, and he uses the move to guard his b pawn with the rook. } 18... Rb8 19. Bd7 Be6 20. b3 { Better for you, but his rook guards the b pawn, and your bishop on d7 wins nothing now. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. f4 { The f pawn strikes at e5, and he takes it: the lines open for him, not for you. } 18... exf4 19. Rhe1 h5 20. Rd5 { Slightly better for you, though a pawn down: his bishop still guards d7. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. Rd5 { The rook steps to d5, but his bishop comes to e6 and hits it. } 18... Be6 19. Rd2 Bxc4 20. Bd7 { Level: his bishop won your c pawn, and your rook never reached his second row. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. Rd8 { Offering a trade on d8 hands the open column away. } 18... Rxd8 19. Bxd8 Bd6 20. Bb6 { Slightly better for him: a pair of rooks came off, and your hold on the open column went with them. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. Rd7+ { Check on his second row, but his bishop simply takes the rook. } 18... Bxd7 19. Bxd7 Ra8 { Level: you gave a rook for his bishop, and your other rook is still at home. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. Rhe1 { The other rook takes the e column, but his bishop now reaches e7, and a rook landing on d7 comes without check. } 18... Be7 19. Bd7 Bxd7 20. Rxd7 { Better for you, but slower: your rook reached his second row without check, and his b pawn is still there. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. g3 { A quiet pawn move, and he uses the time: his rook guards the b pawn from b8. } 18... Rb8 19. Bd7 Bxc2 20. Rd2 { Better for you, but the free move let his bishop grab your pawn on c2. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. Rd2 { Doubling the rooks first is slow: his rook guards the b pawn before your bishop arrives. } 18... Rb8 19. Bd7 Be6 20. Bxe6+ Kxe6 { Better for you, but a pair of bishops came off, and no rook of yours stands on his second row. [%cal Ga4d7] }) (18. Rd6 { The rook runs along the open column, but it stops where his bishop can take it. } 18... Bxd6 { Lost for you: a whole rook for nothing. [%cal Ga4d7] }) 18... Bxd7 { He takes your bishop: anything else loses more. } 19. Rxd7+ { Check, and your rook sits on his second row, hitting his b pawn. [%cal Gd7b7] } 19... Ke6 { His king comes forward and attacks your rook. [%cal Re6d7] } 20. Rxb7 { The rook grabs the b pawn and stays on his second row, out of his king's reach. [%csl Gb7] } (20. Re7+ { Check from e7, but his bishop takes the rook. } 20... Bxe7 { Lost for you: the rook is gone, and so is your grip on his second row. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rhd1 { Guard the rook first and take the pawn next: the same plan in the other order. } 20... g5 21. Rxb7 { Better for you: the same two rooks, one on b7 and one on the open d column. [%csl Gb7] }) (20. Rd3 { The rook steps back off his second row, and his b pawn is safe again. } 20... Rc6 21. Rb3 Rd6 { Level: your rook left his second row, and now his rook takes the open column. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rd2 { The rook retreats to your own second row and gives up the b pawn. } 20... Rc6 21. Ba5 b6 { Level: his rook freed itself on c6, and your bishop was pushed back to a5. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rc7 { The rook stays on his second row, but he simply offers a trade on c7. } 20... Rxc7 21. Bxc7 Be7 { Level: a pair of rooks came off, and his b pawn is safe. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rd5 { The rook drops back to the fifth row, and his rook wakes up at once. } 20... Rc6 21. Ba5 b6 22. Bc3 Rd6 { Level: his rook now fights you for the d column, and the b pawn got away. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rdd1 { The rook runs all the way home, and his bishop comes out with no trouble. } 20... Be7 21. Rd5 Rc6 { Level: all your rook did was go home and come back, while his pieces woke up. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rd8 { Offering a trade on d8 gives up his second row for nothing. } 20... Rxd8 21. Bxd8 Be7 { Level: one pair of rooks is gone, and the b pawn is still his. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rxg7 { The rook grabs the g pawn instead, but his bishop is guarding it. } 20... Bxg7 { Lost for you: the rook is gone for a pawn. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (20. Rd6+ { Check, but the rook stops where his bishop takes it. } 20... Bxd6 { Lost for you: a rook given away for nothing. [%cal Gd7b7] }) 20... Bd6 { He brings his last bishop out. } 21. Rd1 { Your second rook takes the open d column. You are a pawn up, with one rook on his second row and the other hitting his bishop on d6. That is the open column. [%cal Gd1d6][%csl Gb7] } (21. b3 { A pawn move, and his rook comes to b8 to trade yours off his second row. } 21... Rb8 22. Rxb8 Rxb8 23. Ba5 { Better for you, a pawn up, but his rook traded yours off his second row, and your rook on h1 never joined in. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Re1 { The rook comes to the e column, and his rook challenges yours on b8. } 21... Rb8 22. Rxb8 Rxb8 23. Ba5 { Much better for him: his rook took the b column, and your queen side pawns are hanging. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Bxc5 { The bishop grabs the c pawn, but his bishop takes back with check. } 21... Bxc5+ 22. Ke2 { Much better for him: you gave the bishop for a pawn. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. f4 { The f pawn strikes at e5, and his rook comes to b8 at once. } 21... Rb8 22. Rxb8 Rxb8 { Much better for him: his rook traded yours off and now hits your bishop on b6. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Re7+ { Check from e7, but his king takes the rook. } 21... Kxe7 { Lost for you: the rook went for nothing. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Rxg7 { Grabbing the g pawn looks free, but his rook comes to b8 and hits your bishop. } 21... Rb8 22. Bc7 Rxb2 { Level: you took a pawn on g7, and his rook took one on b2 and reached your second row. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Ra7 { The rook eyes his a pawn, but he offers a rook trade on a8. } 21... Ra8 22. Rxa8 Rxa8 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, but one pair of rooks is gone and none of yours sits on his second row. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Ba5 { The bishop steps back, and his rook comes to b8 to meet yours. } 21... Rb8 22. Rb3 h5 { Better for you, a pawn up, but your rook left his second row and your other rook is still on h1. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. Ba7 { The bishop moves, but his rook takes the d column before yours. } 21... Rhd8 22. Rd1 Rd7 { Better for you, but his rook got to the d column first and now challenges yours. [%cal Gh1d1] }) (21. a4 { The a pawn goes forward, but his rook comes to b8 to trade yours off his second row. } 21... Rb8 22. Rxb8 Rxb8 23. a5 Kd7 { Better for you: a pawn up, but one pair of rooks is traded and your other rook is still on h1. [%cal Gh1d1] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: holes in front of the king, St Petersburg 1909"]
[Result "1-0"]
