[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Game 22, line 9730; Forgacz - Tartakower, St Petersburg 1909; from move 19"]
[FEN "r4rk1/3bqppp/2n5/p2pP1N1/1ppP2p1/2P5/PPBQ3P/4RRK1 b - - 1 19"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/3bqppp/2n5/p2pP1N1/1ppP2p1/2P5/PPBQ3P/4RRK1_b_-_-_1_19"]

{ Next, holes in front of the king: squares near his king that none of his pawns can ever guard again, so no pawn can chase your piece off them. St Petersburg, 1909: you have White, two pawns down, and his king is your target. }
19... g6 { His g pawn steps forward, a mistake: the h pawn push was better. Now no pawn of his can ever guard f6: it is a hole. Put your rook in it. [%csl Rf6][%cal Yf1f6] } 20. Rf6 { Your rook lands in the hole, guarded by your pawn on e5, and no pawn of his can ever chase it away. [%cal Ge5f6][%csl Gf6] } (20. h4 { The h pawn marches, and he uses the time to bring his rook over. } 20... bxc3 21. bxc3 Ra6 { Level: while your pawn marched, his rook reached a6, ready to meet a rook on f6. [%cal Gf1f6] }) (20. Nxh7 { The knight sacrifice on h7 opens nothing: his king simply takes it. } 20... Kxh7 21. Rf6 Rg8 { Much better for him: you gave a knight for a pawn, and his rook now guards the g column. [%cal Gf1f6] }) (20. e6 { The pawn hits his bishop, but his f pawn takes it, and the pawn that guarded f6 is gone. } 20... fxe6 21. Ba4 Rxf1+ 22. Rxf1 { Much better for him: a pawn more gone, a pair of rooks traded, and no pawn of yours left to guard f6. [%cal Gf1f6] }) (20. Qf4 { The queen heads for the hole, but his king steps to g7 and guards f6 itself. [%csl Rf6] } 20... Kg7 21. Be4 h6 { Much better for him: your queen got nowhere, and you are still two pawns down. [%cal Gf1f6] }) 20... Ra6 { His rook swings to a6 to fight you for the sixth row. Double up: the other rook to f1. [%cal Ye1f1] } 21. Ref1 { Two rooks on the f column: if he trades on f6, your other rook takes its place in the hole. [%cal Gf1f6] } (21. Qf2 { The queen backs up the rook instead: still good, but after a trade on f6 only a pawn is left in the hole. } 21... Nxd4 22. cxd4 Rxf6 23. exf6 { Better for you, but your rook is gone from f6 and a pawn stands there instead. [%cal Ge1f1] }) (21. h4 { The h pawn marches, and his knight gets time to jump out. } 21... bxc3 22. bxc3 Nb4 { Level: his knight hits your bishop on c2, and your rook on f6 stands alone. [%cal Ge1f1] }) (21. Nxh7 { The knight sacrifice on h7 opens nothing here either. } 21... Kxh7 22. Qf2 Kg8 { Much better for him: the knight is gone for a pawn, and his king went straight back home. [%cal Ge1f1] }) (21. Rxf7 { The rook grabs the f pawn, but his rook takes back and your hole is empty. } 21... Rxf7 22. Nxf7 Qxf7 { Much better for him: after the trades you are a knight down for a pawn. [%cal Ge1f1] }) 21... bxc3 { His b pawn takes on c3 to open lines on the other wing. Take back with the pawn, so your d pawn keeps its guard. [%cal Yb2c3] } 22. bxc3 { The pawn takes back, and your d pawn stays guarded. Now watch your rooks keep the hole. [%tail][%cal Gc3d4] } (22. Qxc3 { The queen takes back, but his knight grabs the pawn on e5, the guard of your rook in the hole. } 22... Nxe5 23. Rxa6 Nd3 { About level: your rook left the hole to take his rook, and his knight took the pawn that guarded f6. [%csl Rf6] }) 22... Nxd4 23. cxd4 Rxf6 24. Rxf6 Kg7 25. Qf4 Be8 26. Qxg4 { Your rook still sits in the hole on f6, and his knight is gone: you are a piece for two pawns up. That is what a hole in front of the king costs. [%csl Gf6] } 1-0


