{ Carlsbad, 1911, and you have White. Your knight gives check, and he has to take it. }
28... Qxf6 { His queen takes your knight, the only sensible reply. His g pawn has left home: which squares near his king can no pawn of his ever guard again? } 29. h4 { The pawn to h4 hits his g pawn. He has to spend a move guarding it, and your knight gets h5 with a threat. } (29. Nh5 { The knight jumps into h5 at once, but his queen simply steps to h6, out of its reach. } 29... Qh6 30. g4 f6 { Better for you, but his f pawn now stands on f6, and that hole is filled. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. Bb3 { The bishop steps to b3, and his queen reaches h6 first. } 29... Qh6 30. Bd5 Ra6 { Better for you, but your h pawn never moved, and his queen sits safely on h6. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. Kh2 { The king steps off the first row, and his queen gets to h6. } 29... Qh6 30. Bb3 Bd7 { Better for you, but his queen on h6 now watches h4, and no hole has been taken. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. Qd2 { The queen comes to d2, but his queen takes h6 first. } 29... Qh6 30. Bb3 Bd7 { Better for you, but with his queen on h6 your h pawn can no longer go forward safely. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. d4 { The d pawn strikes in the centre, but he takes it and your centre falls apart. } 29... exd4 30. e5 Nxe5 { Slightly better for you, though two pawns down: his knight now sits on e5 and your attack has lost its way. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. b4 { The b pawn strikes at c5, far from his king. } 29... cxb4 30. cxb4 Nd4 { Slightly better for you, but his knight landed on d4, and nothing happened near his king. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. c4 { The c pawn strikes at b5, and he simply takes it. } 29... bxc4 30. Kh2 Nd4 { Level: a pawn gone on the other wing, and his knight sits on d4. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (29. Qxg5+ { The queen grabs the g pawn with check, but his queen guards it. } 29... Qxg5 { Lost for you: your queen for a pawn. [%cal Gh3h4] }) 29... h6 { He guards the g pawn with his h pawn. } 30. Nh5 { The knight jumps into h5, a hole no pawn of his can ever guard, and hits his queen. [%cal Gh5f6] } (30. hxg5 { Taking on g5 opens the h column, but his queen takes back and offers a trade. } 30... Qxg5 31. Kf2 Qxc1 32. Rxc1 { About level: the queens are gone, and your attack went with them. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. Kh2 { The king steps aside, and his g pawn takes on h4. } 30... gxh4 31. Nh5 Qg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 { About level: the queens came off, and your knight on h5 has no partner left to attack with. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. Kf2 { The king steps to f2, and his g pawn takes on h4 first. } 30... gxh4 31. Nh5 Qg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 { About level: his queen offered the trade on g5, and without queens the holes do little. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. Bb3 { The bishop steps to b3, and his g pawn takes on h4. } 30... gxh4 31. Nh5 Qg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 { About level: the queens are gone, and his king has nothing more to fear. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. d4 { The d pawn strikes in the centre, but his g pawn takes on h4 first. } 30... gxh4 31. Nh5 Qg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 { Level: the queens are off, and without your queen the holes near his king mean little. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. b4 { The b pawn strikes at c5, and his g pawn takes on h4. } 30... gxh4 31. Nh5 Qg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 { About level: his queen forced the trade on g5, and your attack is over. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. c4 { The c pawn strikes at b5, and his g pawn takes on h4. } 30... gxh4 31. Nh5 Qg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 { About level: the queens came off on g5, and the h pawn you had is gone. [%cal Gg3h5] }) (30. Qxg5+ { The queen grabs the g pawn with check, but his h pawn guards it. } 30... hxg5 { Lost for you: the queen is gone for one pawn. [%cal Gg3h5] }) 30... Qe7 { His queen steps back to e7. } 31. f6 { The pawn walks into the hole on f6, guarded by the knight, and hits his queen again. [%tail][%cal Gh5f6,Gf6e7] } (31. Qd2 { A queen move to d2 lets his f pawn reach f6 and close the hole. } 31... f6 32. Bb3+ Kf8 { Better for you, but his pawn on f6 filled the hole before yours could. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. Rf3 { The rook swings to f3, but his f pawn steps to f6 and closes the hole. } 31... f6 32. Bb3+ Kf8 { Better for you, but his pawn now sits on f6, and your knight on h5 has no partner there. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. Kf2 { A king step to f2 gives him the move, and he breaks with his c pawn. } 31... c4 32. dxc4 bxc4 33. hxg5 { Better for you, a pawn up, but his f pawn is still free to close the hole. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. Bd1 { The bishop drops back to d1, and his f pawn steps to f6. } 31... f6 32. Bb3+ Kf8 { Better for you, but his pawn on f6 closes the hole, and your bishop only gave a check. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. Bb3 { The bishop steps to b3, and his c pawn comes forward to chase it. } 31... c4 32. Bc2 b4 { Better for you, but his pawns are rolling on the other wing, and your pawn is still on f5. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. hxg5 { Taking on g5 looks natural, but his queen takes back and offers a trade. } 31... Qxg5 32. Qxg5+ hxg5 33. Kh2 { Better for you, but the queens are gone and his king can breathe again. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. Nf6+ { The knight check just gives the knight away: his queen takes it. } 31... Qxf6 32. hxg5 Qxg5 { Much better for him: a knight down, and his queen took your pawn on g5 as well. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. Qxg5+ { The queen takes on g5 with check, but his h pawn takes her. } 31... hxg5 { Lost for you: a queen for a single pawn. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. d4 { The d pawn strikes in the centre, and he takes it at once. } 31... exd4 32. e5 Nxe5 33. cxd4 { Level: you gave the e pawn, and his knight now stands in the centre instead of your pawns. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. b4 { The b pawn hits c5, on the wrong wing. } 31... cxb4 32. f6 Qa7+ { Level: his queen escaped with check, and your b pawn is gone. [%cal Gf5f6] }) (31. c4 { The c pawn strikes at b5, and his knight jumps to d4. } 31... Nd4 32. Bd1 bxc4 33. f6 { Level: your pawn reached f6 a move late, and pushing your c pawn let his knight into d4. [%csl Gf6] }) 31... Qf8 32. Ng7 { Your pawn on f6 and your knight on g7 sit in holes no pawn of his can ever touch, right beside his king. That is what holes cost him. [%csl Gf6,Gg7] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: the backward pawn, Havana 1913"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/lwQ49VLO/BYQJZyD4"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Game 26, line 10247; Capablanca - Blanco, Havana 1913; from move 12"]
[FEN "r1bqnrk1/pp2bppp/2p1p3/4N1B1/3P4/2PB3Q/PP3PPP/R3K2R b KQ - 6 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqnrk1/pp2bppp/2p1p3/4N1B1/3P4/2PB3Q/PP3PPP/R3K2R_b_KQ_-_6_12"]

{ Next, the backward pawn: a pawn none of his other pawns can ever guard, with the square in front of it held by you. Havana, 1913: Capablanca, world champion from 1921, had White, and so do you. }
12... f5 { He pushes his f pawn to f5 to shut out your bishop, a reasonable move. But now his e pawn is backward: no pawn of his can ever guard it. First take his dark bishop. [%csl Re6][%cal Yg5e7] } 13. Bxe7 { His dark bishop hit yours, and it was his only bishop that could ever fight your knight on e5. His queen must take back and stand guard. Now watch your rooks line up. [%tail][%cal Gd4e5] } (13. Be3 { The bishop steps back, and his dark bishop stays on the board to fight for the squares around e6. } 13... Bf6 14. O-O-O Nc7 { Better for you, but his bishop on f6 is alive and hits your knight on e5. [%cal Gg5e7] }) (13. Bf4 { The bishop steps back to f4, and his dark bishop lives on. } 13... Nf6 14. O-O-O Nd5 { Better for you, but his knight reached d5 and his bishop still guards the dark squares. [%cal Gg5e7] }) (13. Bd2 { The bishop retreats all the way to d2, and he frees his game with the c pawn. } 13... c5 14. dxc5 Bxc5 { Slightly better for you, but his pawn trade let his bishop out to c5. [%cal Gg5e7] }) (13. O-O { Castling leaves your bishop on g5 hanging: his bishop takes it. } 13... Bxg5 { Much better for him: a whole bishop for nothing. [%cal Gg5e7] }) (13. O-O-O { Castling long leaves your bishop on g5 hanging, and his bishop takes it with check. } 13... Bxg5+ { Much better for him: a bishop lost, and check as well. [%cal Gg5e7] }) 13... Qxe7 14. O-O Rf6 15. Rfe1 Nd6 16. Re2 Bd7 17. Rae1 { Two rooks behind your knight on e5 aim at the backward pawn on e6, and his queen, rook and bishop are all tied to guarding it. That is a backward pawn. [%csl Re6][%cal Ge1e5] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: the backward pawn, one more attacker"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/lwQ49VLO/D95t0Kch"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Diagram 92, line 5767"]
[FEN "3r2k1/2qrbppp/pp1p4/2p5/2P1PB2/1P1R4/P2Q1PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r2k1/2qrbppp/pp1p4/2p5/2P1PB2/1P1R4/P2Q1PPP/3R2K1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ His pawn on d6 is backward, and his pieces guard it exactly as often as yours attack it. Can you add an attacker he cannot answer? }
1. e5 { The e pawn attacks the backward pawn, and taking on d6 would hit his queen and his bishop at once. Now watch it fall. [%tail][%cal Ge5d6] } (1. Bg3 { The bishop steps back, and he closes e5 with his f pawn. } 1... f6 { Better for you, but his pawn on f6 now takes e5 away from your e pawn. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. Rd5 { The rook steps to d5, in front of the pawn, but he gets time for f6. } 1... f6 2. h4 Bf8 { Better for you, but his f pawn now guards e5, and your e pawn cannot step there without being taken. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. h3 { A breathing hole for your king, but he uses the move to push his f pawn. } 1... f6 2. Rd5 Bf8 { Better for you, but his pawn on f6 covers e5, so the backward pawn is safe from your e pawn. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. a4 { The a pawn goes forward, far from the pawn on d6. } 1... f6 2. Rd5 Qc6 { Better for you, but his f pawn closed e5, so your e pawn has no safe step forward. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. g3 { A quiet pawn move, and he answers with f6. } 1... f6 2. Rd5 Qc6 { Better for you, but with his pawn on f6 no pawn of yours can safely attack d6. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. b4 { The b pawn strikes at c5, but he takes it and your queen side loosens. } 1... cxb4 2. Rd4 Qc8 { Level: you are a pawn down, and the backward pawn stands untouched. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. Bxd6 { The bishop takes on d6, but four of his pieces guard it. } 1... Rxd6 { Much better for him: you gave a bishop for a pawn. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. Rxd6 { The rook takes on d6, but four of his pieces guard it, and your attackers run out first. } 1... Bxd6 2. Bxd6 Qxd6 3. Qxd6 Rxd6 4. Rxd6 Rxd6 { Much better for him: after all the trades on d6 you are a rook down for a pawn. [%cal Ge4e5] }) (1. Rd4 { The rook steps to d4, where his c pawn takes it. } 1... cxd4 2. Qxd4 { Much better for him: you gave a rook for a pawn. [%cal Ge4e5] }) 1... Qc6 2. exd6 { A pawn up: your e pawn captured the backward pawn, and from d6 it now hits his bishop. A backward pawn cannot run from a pawn. [%csl Gd6] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: the lead in development, St Petersburg 1914"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/lwQ49VLO/Xu1gYoQ8"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Game 43, line 12566; Capablanca - Bernstein, St Petersburg 1914; from move 16"]
[FEN "r1b1k2r/2qn2pp/p1p5/1pb1p3/1P2P1n1/2NB1NB1/P4PPP/2RQ1RK1 b kq - 0 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1k2r/2qn2pp/p1p5/1pb1p3/1P2P1n1/2NB1NB1/P4PPP/2RQ1RK1_b_kq_-_0_16"]

{ Next, the lead in development: more of your pieces out and working than his. St Petersburg, 1914: José Raúl Capablanca, world champion from 1921, had White, and so do you. }
16... Ba7 { His bishop steps back, a slow move: six of your pieces are working against four of his, and his king still sits on e8. Open lines now: take on b5! [%cal Yd3b5][%csl Re8] } 17. Bxb5 { A bishop for pawns, and worth it: the lines you open run straight at his king, while his rooks and one bishop have not moved yet. [%cal Gd3b5] } (17. h3 { The pawn hits his knight: good too, but it gives him time, and he hands the knight back to free his bishop. } 17... Ne3 18. fxe3 Bxe3+ 19. Kh2 { Much better for you, a piece for a pawn up, but his bishop is out and his king may still castle. [%cal Gd3b5] }) (17. Be2 { The bishop steps back, safe and slow, and his king uses the time to castle. } 17... O-O 18. Nd5 Qd8 { Much better for you, but his king castled out of the middle, and catching it there was the whole point. [%cal Gd3b5] }) (17. a4 { The a pawn strikes at b5, but his queen gets time to come back. } 17... Qd8 18. axb5 axb5 { Much better for you, but his queen is home and your bishop is still waiting on d3. [%cal Gd3b5] }) (17. Nxb5 { The knight takes first: after his pawn takes back, your bishop recaptures, but now it is your knight that is gone. } 17... axb5 18. Bxb5 Bb7 { Much better for you, but you gave your best knight, and his bishop got out to b7. [%cal Gd3b5] }) (17. Nxe5 { The knight grabs the e pawn, but his other knight can simply take it back. } 17... Ndxe5 18. Bxb5 O-O { Much better for him: a knight lost, and his king castled. [%cal Gd3b5] }) 17... axb5 { He takes the bishop, natural but not his toughest: his bishop to b7 held out longer. Take back with the knight: it jumps toward his king. [%cal Yc3b5] } 18. Nxb5 { The knight takes back and hits his queen, and it eyes d6, where it would check his king. [%cal Gb5c7,Gb5d6] } (18. Ng5 { The other knight jumps at his king, but the pawn on b5 stays, and your knight on c3 waits. } 18... Ndf6 19. Nxb5 Qd7 { Better for you, but with a move lost your knight on b5 no longer hits his queen. [%cal Gc3b5] }) (18. a4 { The a pawn attacks b5, but you gave a bishop and need speed: he castles. } 18... O-O 19. Nxb5 Qb8 { Slightly better for you: his king castled, and all you have for your bishop is two pawns. [%cal Gc3b5] }) (18. h3 { The pawn hits his knight, but he gives it back on e3 and frees his game. } 18... Ne3 19. fxe3 Bxe3+ 20. Kh2 { Level: his knight gave itself up to free his bishop, and your attack is over. [%cal Gc3b5] }) (18. Qxd7+ { The queen grabs a knight with check, but his queen takes yours. } 18... Qxd7 { Lost for you: your queen for a knight. [%cal Gc3b5] }) (18. Nxe5 { Taking on e5 with the knight loses it: his other knight takes back. } 18... Ndxe5 19. Nxb5 Qd7 { Better for him: the knight went for a single pawn, and his queen is safe. [%cal Gc3b5] }) 18... Qd8 { His queen runs back to d8. Now the check on d6. [%cal Yb5d6] } 19. Nd6+ { Check from d6: his king has to walk, and he can never castle now. [%csl Re8] } (19. Rxc6 { The rook grabs the c pawn at once, but it lets his king castle. } 19... O-O 20. Qd5+ Kh8 { Much better for you, but his king got out of the middle, and the check on d6 never came. [%cal Gb5d6] }) (19. Nxa7 { The knight takes his bishop, but his rook takes back, and his king is still free to castle. } 19... Rxa7 20. h3 Ngf6 { Much better for you, but the trade brought his rook out, and his king may still castle. [%cal Gb5d6] }) (19. Bh4 { The bishop hits his queen, but he blocks with his knight. } 19... Ndf6 20. Qxd8+ Kxd8 21. Nxa7 { Much better for you, but the queens are off, and his king feels safer without them. [%cal Gb5d6] }) (19. Nc7+ { The knight checks from c7, but his queen takes it. } 19... Qxc7 { Much better for him: a knight thrown away. [%cal Gb5d6] }) (19. Qxd7+ { The queen takes a knight with check, but his queen takes back. } 19... Qxd7 { Lost for you: you traded your queen for a knight. [%cal Gb5d6] }) (19. Nxe5 { Taking on e5 with the other knight loses it. } 19... Ndxe5 20. Qxd8+ Kxd8 21. Nxa7 { Better for him: a knight down, and the queens are off. [%cal Gb5d6] }) 19... Kf8 { His king steps to f8. Now the rook takes the c pawn. [%cal Yc1c6] } 20. Rxc6 { The rook takes on c6: material is level again, his king is on f8, and four of his pieces are back on their home squares. Now watch your knights collect. [%tail][%cal Gc1c6] } (20. h3 { The pawn chases his knight first. Strong too, but his other knight gets to f6 in time. } 20... Ndf6 21. hxg4 Bxg4 22. Rxc6 { Much better for you: the same result as taking at once, a move later. [%cal Gc1c6] }) (20. Qb3 { The queen comes out first, just as strong, threatening mate on f7, and his queen steps to e7 to stop it. } 20... Qe7 21. Rxc6 Nb6 { Much better for you: his queen had to leave d8, and the c pawn still falls, a move late. [%cal Gc1c6] }) (20. Nxc8 { The knight takes his bishop, and his rook takes back: your best piece is gone for his worst. } 20... Rxc8 { Much better for you, but the knight on d6 was the piece that held his king in place. [%cal Gc1c6] }) (20. Nxe5 { Taking on e5 loses a knight: his knight takes back. } 20... Ndxe5 21. h3 h5 { About level: you gave a knight for one more pawn, and his knights now hold the centre. [%cal Gc1c6] }) (20. b5 { The b pawn pushes on, but his c pawn stays, and his knight finds c5. } 20... Nc5 21. h3 h5 { Much better for you, but his knight reached c5 and the c column is still closed. [%cal Gc1c6] }) 20... Nb6 21. Nxe5 Be6 22. Nxg4 { Four pawns up now, and his rook on h8 has still never moved. That is the lead in development: your pieces got there first. [%csl Rh8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: the lead in development, five pieces at home"]
