{ The lead in development again. Your king is castled and four of your pieces are out, while he has moved his queen twice and pushed two edge pawns. Can you make it count? }
8. Qb3 { His queen moves a third time to hit your f7 pawn, a mistake: developing a knight was better. His king and five of his pieces are still at home. [%csl Rf7] } 8... d5 { The pawn to d5 shuts his bishop's line to f7 and hits the bishop at the same time. [%cal Gd5c4] } (8... Kh8 { The king steps into the corner, but it does not save the f pawn. } 9. Bxf7 Rf8 10. Nf3 { About level: he took the f pawn and got his knight out at last. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Ne5 { The knight hits his bishop too, but his queen and bishop still aim at f7. } 9. Nc3 Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxc4 11. Qxc4 d5 12. Qe2 dxe4 { Much better for you, a pawn up, but the pawn to d5 at once would have won a whole piece. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Rf8 { The rook goes back to guard f7, undoing its own development. [%csl Gf7] } 9. Bd5 d6 10. Nf3 { About level: he got his knight out, and your lead is gone. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Nd4 { The knight hits his queen, but his bishop takes on f7 with check first. } 9. Bxf7+ Kf8 10. Qc4 { About level: a pawn down, and your king had to walk. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Kf8 { The king steps to f8, but it guarded f7 already: a strange way to use your lead. [%csl Gf7] } 9. Bxf7 Nd4 10. Qc4 { About level: he took the f pawn anyway, and your king left its corner for nothing. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Nxe4 { Taking the e pawn looks free, but his bishop takes on f7 with check. } 9. Bxf7+ Kh8 10. Bxe8 Qxe8 { About level: you lost the exchange, and the attack is gone. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Bb4+ { The bishop checks, but his a pawn simply takes it. } 9. axb4 d5 10. Be2 { Better for him: the bishop is gone for nothing, and his pieces are coming out. [%cal Gd7d5] }) (8... Bxa3 { The bishop grabs the a pawn, but his bishop takes on f7 with check and then your rook. } 9. Bxf7+ Kh8 10. Bxe8 Bb4+ 11. c3 Bc5 12. Bf7 { Better for him: his bishop took a whole rook and got away to f7, and you won only a pawn. [%cal Gd7d5] }) 9. Bxd5 { His bishop takes on d5. } 9... Nxd5 { A piece for a pawn, and the knight on d5 is safe: if he takes it, your bishop and rook give check together along the open lines. That is the lead in development. [%cal Ge7b4,Ge8e1] } (9... Qxd5 { The queen takes back: just as good, and the queens come off. } 10. Qxd5 Nxd5 { Much better for you: a piece for a pawn, the same win with the queens gone. [%csl Gd5] }) (9... Nd4 { The knight hits his queen, but his bishop takes on f7 with check. } 10. Bxf7+ Kf8 11. Qd3 Kxf7 { Better for you: the bishop is yours, but you paid two pawns and your king walked. [%cal Gf6d5] }) (9... Bb4+ { The bishop checks, but his a pawn takes it, and you only take back the bishop. } 10. axb4 Nxd5 11. Ne2 { Better for you, but your dark bishop is gone, and you are a pawn down again. [%cal Gf6d5] }) (9... Bc5 { The bishop comes out, but his bishop is still on d5, and it takes on f7 with check. } 10. Bxf7+ Kh8 11. Be3 Bxe3 12. fxe3 { Better for you, but you are two pawns down: his bishop on d5 was yours to take. [%cal Gf6d5] }) (9... Nxe4 { The knight takes the e pawn instead, and his bishop takes the knight. } 10. Bxe4 Bf5 11. Nd2 Bxe4 12. Nxe4 Bh4 13. Be3 Rxe4 14. Nf3 { Better for you, but after all that the material is only level: taking his bishop on d5 was simpler. [%cal Gf6d5] }) (9... Bxa3 { The bishop grabs the a pawn, and his bishop takes on f7 with check. } 10. Bxf7+ Kh8 11. Nxa3 { Much better for him: you lost a bishop and two pawns. [%cal Gf6d5] }) (9... Bxh3 { The bishop takes on h3, but his bishop takes on f7 with check first. } 10. Bxf7+ Kh8 11. Nxh3 { Lost for you: a bishop gone for a pawn, and his bishop still sits on f7. [%cal Gf6d5] }) 0-1


