[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/1bn2n2/1B2p3/1P2P3/2P2N2/P2P2PP/RNBQK2R_w_KQkq_-_3_8"]

{ Carlsbad, 1911, and you have Black. Both kings still stand in the middle. }
8. Nxe5 { His knight grabs your e pawn, a mistake: the a pawn push was better. His king still stands on e1. } 8... O-O { Castle first! Your king is safe and your rook comes toward the e column, while his king stays in the middle. The pawn can wait. [%tail][%cal Ge8g8] } (8... Nxe4 { Taking back the pawn at once is natural, but his queen jumps out first. } 9. Qf3 { Better for you, but his queen now hits f7 and your knight on e4 while your king is still in the middle. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... Bd7 { The bishop steps in to guard your knight, but his knight takes it. } 9. Nxd7 Qxd7 10. d4 { Level: a pawn down, and your lead has melted away. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... a6 { The a pawn asks his bishop to decide, and it takes your knight. } 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. d4 { Level: a pawn down with split pawns, and his centre stands. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... a5 { The a pawn hits his b pawn, but he takes on c6 first. } 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc6+ Bd7 11. Bxa8 Qxa8 { About level: you are the exchange and two pawns down, but his king is still on e1. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... Qd6 { The queen hits his knight, but his d pawn steps forward to guard it. } 9. d4 O-O 10. Nc4 { About level: his centre stands, and your queen found nothing to attack. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... Bf2+ { The bishop check wins nothing: his king takes it. } 9. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 10. Kg1 { Better for him: you gave a bishop for a pawn, and his king found a corner. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (8... Qxd2+ { The queen grabs the d pawn with check, but his knight takes her. } 9. Nxd2 { Lost for you: the queen went for one pawn. [%cal Ge8g8] }) 9. Nf3 Nxe4 { The pawn is back, your king is castled, and his king cannot castle at all: your bishop on b6 covers g1. That is what a lead in development buys. [%cal Gb6g1] } 0-1


