{ Vienna, 1910, and you have White. }
7... Qxe5 { His queen takes back the pawn on e5, his best move here. Your move. } 8. O-O-O { Castle long! Your king steps out of the pin on the e column, and your rook lines up on the d column behind your bishop. [%cal Gd1d8] } (8. f3 { The pawn guards your knight, but he trades it off, and the queens come off too. } 8... Nxe4 9. Qxe4 Qxe4+ 10. fxe4 { Level: the queens are gone, and so is your lead. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (8. Nf3 { The knight develops, but your knight on e4 is pinned, and his queen takes it with check. } 8... Qxe4+ 9. Qxe4+ Nxe4 { Much better for him: a knight down, and the queens are gone. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (8. Be3 { The bishop develops and blocks the e column, but your knight on e4 is attacked twice and falls. } 8... Qxe4 9. Qxe4+ Nxe4 { Much better for him: he won a whole knight. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (8. Be2 { The bishop steps in front of your king, but his queen still takes the knight. } 8... Qxe4 9. O-O-O Be6 { Much better for him: a knight down, even with your king castled. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (8. Rd1 { The rook takes the d column, but your king is still on e1, and the pinned knight falls with check. } 8... Qxe4+ 9. Qxe4+ Nxe4 { Much better for him: the queens are off, and he is a piece up. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (8. Qd8+ { The queen sacrifice comes too early: your king is still on e1, so no rook stands on d1 for a double check. } 8... Kxd8 { Lost for you: a queen given for nothing. [%cal Ge1c1] }) (8. Qd7+ { The queen checks on d7, but his knight takes her. } 8... Nbxd7 { Lost for you: the queen is gone for good. [%cal Ge1c1] }) 8... Nxe4 { He grabs your knight, and that was the mistake: his bishop to e7 would have held. } 9. Qd8+ { The queen sacrifice! His king must take, and your bishop gets a double check. [%cal Gd3d8] } (9. Be3 { The bishop develops, but his queen steps to d5 and offers a trade. } 9... Qd5 10. Qxd5 cxd5 11. Rxd5 { Much better for him: the queens came off, and you are a knight down for a pawn. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. f4 { The pawn hits his queen, but she steps to d5 and keeps the knight. } 9... Qd5 10. Qe2 f5 { Much better for him: he is a knight up, and his pieces hold the centre. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. Re1 { The rook pins his knight against his queen, and you win it back. } 9... Bf5 10. g4 Be6 11. Rxe4 { Slightly better for you: the knight is back, but his king got away with it. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. Qxe4 { The queen takes back the knight, but his queen takes yours. } 9... Qxe4 10. Re1 Qxe1+ 11. Bxe1 { Lost for you: the queens came off, and you are a whole rook down. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. Nf3 { The knight develops, but his queen just steps away and grabs a pawn. } 9... Qd5 10. Qe2 Qxa2 { Better for him: a knight up, and his queen is now close to your king. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. Qe2 { The queen steps onto the e column to pin his knight, but he guards it and develops. } 9... Qe7 10. Be3 Be6 { Much better for him: he stays a knight up, and his king is safe. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. h4 { A pawn move gives him time, and his knight takes your bishop. } 9... Nxd2 10. Qxd2 Be7 { Much better for him: a knight up, and his bishop is out. [%cal Gd3d8] }) (9. Qd7+ { The queen checks from d7, but his knight takes her. } 9... Nxd7 { Lost for you: a queen thrown away, and no mate follows. [%cal Gd3d8] }) 9... Kxd8 { His king has to take the queen. } 10. Bg5+ { Double check: the bishop checks from g5 and the rook from d1, and no single move can block both. [%cal Gg5d8,Gd1d8] } (10. Re1 { The rook swings to the e column, but it gives no check, and his king gets time. } 10... Ba3 11. c3 Qd5 { Lost for you: a queen down, and his pieces come to life. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Bf4+ { Check, and the bishop hits his queen too, but his queen blocks on d5. } 10... Qd5 11. Rxd5+ cxd5 { Lost for you: your rook took his queen, but his pawn took the rook. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Bc3+ { The bishop steps to c3 and your rook gives the check, but his queen blocks on d6. } 10... Qd6 11. Be1 Kc7 { Lost for you: a queen down, and his king is safe on c7. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Be3+ { Your rook checks once the bishop leaves for e3, but his knight blocks on d7. } 10... Nd7 11. Nf3 Qe6 { Lost for you: the knight block holds, and you are a queen down. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Be1+ { The bishop steps back to e1 and your rook checks, and his king walks to c7. } 10... Kc7 11. Nf3 Qf4+ { Lost for you: his king is out, and now his queen checks yours. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Bh6+ { The bishop runs to h6 and only your rook checks: his knight blocks on d7. } 10... Nd7 11. Be3 Ba3 { Lost for you: one check was not enough, and his pieces come out. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Bb4+ { The bishop goes to b4, so your rook checks, and his king steps to c7. } 10... Kc7 11. Bxf8 Nxf2 { Lost for you: you won his bishop, but his knight takes on f2 and forks your rooks. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Ba5+ { A double check from bishop and rook, but his king steps back to e8, off both lines. } 10... Ke8 11. Bd3 Qxa5 { Lost for you: his queen takes the bishop, and his king is safe. [%cal Gd2g5] }) (10. Nf3 { The knight develops, but it gives no check, and his king gets away. } 10... Qe8 11. Ba5+ Ke7 { Lost for you: the moment for the double check has passed. [%cal Gd2g5] }) 10... Kc7 { His king runs to c7. } 11. Bd8# { Mate: the bishop checks from d8, your rook guards it, and every square around his king is covered or blocked by his own men. Five of his pieces never left home: that is the lead in development. [%cal Gd1d8] } (11. Rd6 { The rook goes to d6, but his queen takes your bishop with check. } 11... Qxg5+ 12. Kb1 Bxd6 { Lost for you: bishop and rook gone, and the mate with them. [%csl Gd8] }) (11. Rd8 { The rook goes to d8, but it gives no check, and his queen takes your bishop. } 11... Qxg5+ 12. Kb1 Qxd8 { Lost for you: both your pieces are gone, and his king is safe. [%csl Gd8] }) (11. Rd7+ { The rook check looks fierce, but his knight takes the rook. } 11... Nxd7 { Lost for you: the rook is gone, and so is the mate on d8. [%csl Gd8] }) (11. Nf3 { The knight develops, but it gives no check, and his queen steps away. } 11... Qe8 12. Be3 Bg4 { Lost for you: the mate on d8 was there, and now his pieces are out. [%csl Gd8] }) (11. f4 { The pawn hits his queen, but it gives no check, and she steps to e8. } 11... Qe8 12. Bh4 Be6 { Lost for you: a queen down, and the chance to mate is gone. [%csl Gd8] }) (11. Be3 { The bishop steps back, and his king slips out of the net. } 11... Ba3 12. Bd4 Qg5+ { Lost for you: his queen is free, and the mate slipped away. [%csl Gd8] }) (11. Nh3 { The knight comes out, and his bishop takes it. } 11... Bxh3 12. Be3 Be6 { Lost for you: a queen and two knights down, with no mate left. [%csl Gd8] }) 1-0


