{ Last, the two bishops: one rules the light squares, the other the dark, so together they reach every square. Pistyan, 1912, and you have Black. }
15. Bxf5 { His bishop takes your knight, his best move: it was about to jump to g3 with check. Take back with the bishop. [%cal Yc8f5] } 15... Bxf5 { Now you have both bishops and he has one. They point at his king from both colours. [%cal Gd4g1,Gf5h3] } (15... gxf5 { Taking back with the pawn keeps both bishops, but the pawn on f5 blocks your light bishop's road to his king. } 16. Re1+ Kf8 17. h3 { Level: two bishops, but one of them is shut in behind your own pawn. [%cal Gc8f5] }) (15... Bxc3 { Taking his knight gives up your dark bishop, and his bishop on f5 takes your other bishop first. } 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 17. bxc3 { Much better for him: you are a piece down, and he has the only bishop left. [%cal Gc8f5] }) (15... O-O { Castling leaves his bishop on f5 free, and it takes your bishop on c8. } 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 17. f5 { Much better for him: you are a knight down after all. [%cal Gc8f5] }) 16. Re1+ { He checks along the open e column. Step your king to f8: castling is gone, but your rook wants the h column anyway. [%cal Ye8f8] } 16... Kf8 { The king steps aside, and your rook on h8 stays right behind your h pawn. [%csl Gh8] } (16... Kd7 { The king walks toward the other wing, a long trip with his queen watching. } 17. Qb3 Qb6 18. Qa4+ Kc8 { About level: your king made a long trip to c8, and your attack never started. [%cal Ge8f8] }) (16... Qe7 { The queen blocks the check, but his rook on e1 now pins her to your king, and his knight joins the attack from b5. } 17. Nb5 h3 18. Rxe7+ Kxe7 { Much better for him: your queen went for a rook. [%cal Ge8f8] }) (16... Be6 { The bishop blocks the check, but his pawn takes it. } 17. dxe6 Bxc3 18. bxc3 { Much better for him: both your bishops are gone, and he kept his own. [%cal Ge8f8] }) 17. Qf3 { His queen comes to f3, a mistake: the pawn to h3 was needed, to stop yours. Push your h pawn. [%cal Yh4h3] } 17... h3 { The pawn to h3 hits his g pawn and pries open his king's cover, and your bishops get their diagonals. [%cal Gh3g2] } (17... a6 { A useful little move, but he gets time for the pawn to h3 himself. } 18. h3 Qd7 19. a4 { Slightly better for you: his pawn reached h3 first, and your h pawn is stuck on h4. [%cal Gh4h3] }) (17... Qd7 { The queen heads for the light squares, a good idea but a move too early: he stops your pawn first. } 18. h3 a6 19. a4 { Slightly better for you, but his pawn on h3 now holds yours back. [%cal Gh4h3] }) (17... Kg7 { The king steps to g7, and he uses the move to close the h pawn's road. } 18. h3 Re8 19. Rxe8 Qxe8 { Slightly better for you: a pair of rooks came off, and his pawn on h3 froze yours. [%cal Gh4h3] }) (17... Qb6 { The queen hits his b pawn, and his knight jumps to b5. } 18. Nb5 Bf6 19. Bd2 { Slightly better for you, but your queen went the wrong way, and your h pawn never moved. [%cal Gh4h3] }) (17... Bxc3 { Taking his knight gives up your dark bishop, and the pair with it. } 18. Qxc3 f6 19. h3 { About level: one bishop each now, and his king got its air. [%cal Gh4h3] }) (17... b5 { The b pawn offers itself to open lines on the other wing, and his knight takes it. } 18. Nxb5 h3 19. g3 { About level: you gave a pawn, and your h pawn came a move late. [%cal Gh4h3] }) 18. g3 { He pushes the g pawn to g3, and the light squares around his king have no pawn left to guard them. Bring your queen to d7. [%cal Yd8d7] } 18... Qd7 { Your queen lines up behind your light bishop, and his king cannot move at all: your dark bishop covers g1, and the light one guards the pawn that covers g2. That is the two bishops at work. [%cal Gd4g1,Gf5h3,Gh3g2] } (18... a6 { A careful little move, but it leaves your queen at home. } 19. Bd2 Qd7 20. Ne4 { Better for you, but your queen came a move late, and his knight found e4. [%cal Gd8d7] }) (18... Kg7 { The king goes to g7, but his knight jumps to b5 first. } 19. Nb5 Qf6 20. Nc3 { Better for you, but his knight is back, and your queen stands on the dark squares instead of the light ones. [%cal Gd8d7] }) (18... Qc8 { The queen heads for the light squares by a slower road. } 19. Nb5 Bg4 20. Qf1 { Better for you, but his queen got back to guard the light squares first. [%cal Gd8d7] }) (18... Bxc3 { Taking his knight gives up the dark bishop, the one that covers g1. [%cal Rd4g1] } 19. Qxc3 f6 20. b3 { Level: one bishop each, and his king has g1 back. [%cal Gd8d7] }) 0-1


