{ Amsterdam, 1889, and you have White, like Emanuel Lasker, world champion from 1894, not Edward Lasker who wrote these plans. His king has only a knight and pawns around it. }
14... Nxh5 { He takes your knight on h5, a mistake: his rook to c8 was better. Your move. } 15. Bxh7+ { The first bishop crashes in on h7 with check. } (15. Qxh5 { The queen simply takes back the knight. Level on material, but your bishops never strike. } 15... f5 16. Rf3 Qe8 17. Qxe8 Raxe8 { Level: the queens are gone, and so is any attack on his king. [%cal Gd3h7] }) (15. Bxg7 { The wrong bishop first: his knight takes it back from h5. } 15... Nxg7 16. f5 e5 { Lost for you: two pieces gone for a pawn, and his king is well guarded. [%cal Gd3h7] }) (15. Bxa6 { The bishop grabs a pawn on the wrong wing, and he takes it. } 15... Bxa6 16. Qxh5 Bxf1 17. Rxf1 { Lost for you: a bishop and the exchange gone, and his king is safe. [%cal Gd3h7] }) (15. f5 { The f pawn hits his e pawn, but his knight simply goes back to f6. } 15... Nf6 { Lost for you: a knight down, and his king has its guard back. [%cal Gd3h7] }) 15... Kxh7 { His king takes the bishop. } 16. Qxh5+ { Your queen takes the knight back with check. } (16. Rf3 { The rook swings to f3 first, but his knight is still on h5, and his rook takes the h column. } 16... Rh8 17. Rh3 Kg8 18. Rxh5 { Much better for him: you got the knight back, but you are a bishop down for a pawn, and his king is safe. [%cal Ge2h5] }) (16. Qg4 { The queen steps to g4 without check, and he guards with g6. } 16... g6 17. f5 exf5 { Lost for you: a bishop and a knight down, and his king is safe. [%cal Ge2h5] }) (16. Qd3+ { Check from d3, but his king steps back to g8, and he keeps the knight. } 16... Kg8 17. Qe2 d4 { Lost for you: a bishop and a knight down for a pawn. [%cal Ge2h5] }) 16... Kg8 { His king runs back to g8. } 17. Bxg7 { The second bishop crashes in on g7. If his king takes, your queen checks from g4 and your rook swings over. } (17. Rf3 { The rook comes first, but he closes the door with f6. } 17... f6 18. Bb2 Qe8 19. Qh3 { Much better for him: a bishop down for a pawn, and his f pawn cut your other bishop off from his king. [%cal Ge5g7] }) (17. Rf2 { The rook steps to f2, but he blocks your bishop with f6. } 17... f6 18. Bb2 Qe8 19. Qh3 { Much better for him: his f pawn shut your bishop out, and you are a piece down for a pawn. [%cal Ge5g7] }) (17. Qh3 { The queen stays on the h column, but he blocks your bishop with f6. } 17... f6 18. Bb2 d4 19. Rf3 { Much better for him: your bishop is cut off, and you have only a pawn for your piece. [%cal Ge5g7] }) (17. f5 { The f pawn joins, but he blocks your bishop with f6 first. } 17... f6 18. Bf4 d4 19. Rf2 { Lost for you: a piece down for a pawn, and his king is safe behind his pawns. [%cal Ge5g7] }) 17... Kxg7 { His king takes the second bishop. } 18. Qg4+ { Check from g4, and the rook on f1 gets its road to the h column. } (18. Rf3 { The rook comes without check, and his rook runs to g8 to guard his king. } 18... Rg8 19. Rg3+ Kf8 20. Qh6+ { About level: you have checks, but his king escaped toward f8. [%cal Gh5g4] }) (18. Qe5+ { Check from e5, but his bishop blocks on f6. } 18... Bf6 19. Qh5 Rh8 20. Qg4+ { Lost for you: the check came one move late, and his rook now guards the h column. [%cal Gh5g4] }) (18. f5 { The f pawn comes forward without check: he takes it, and then brings his rook to the h column. } 18... exf5 19. Rf3 Rh8 20. Qxf5 { Lost for you: his rook reached the h column first, and you are two bishops down for two pawns. [%cal Gh5g4] }) 18... Kh7 { His king runs to h7. } 19. Rf3 { The rook heads for h3, to check along the h column. } (19. Qh5+ { Another check first: it keeps the attack, but he just goes back to g7. } 19... Kg7 20. Qg4+ { Much better for you: the same position again, so the rook still has to come. [%cal Gf1f3] }) (19. Qh3+ { A check from h3 keeps the attack going, but his king simply steps back to g7. } 19... Kg7 20. Qg3+ Kh8 { Much better for you, but you only went round in a circle: the rook still has to come. [%cal Gf1f3] }) (19. f5 { The f pawn joins without check, and his queen gets to g6. } 19... exf5 20. Qxf5+ Qg6 { Lost for you: his queen blocked the check, and you gave two pieces for two pawns. [%cal Gf1f3] }) 19... e5 { He opens a road for his queen to come and block. } 20. Rh3+ { Your rook checks along the h column, and his king has no square to step to. } (20. Raf1 { The other rook comes to f1, but it gives no check, and his queen gets to g6. } 20... Qg6 21. Qh3+ Qh6 22. fxe5 { Level: his queen came back to guard his king, and the attack ran dry. [%cal Gf3h3] }) (20. Qf5+ { Check from f5, but his queen blocks on g6, and the rook check on the h column only drives his king away. } 20... Qg6 21. Rh3+ Kg7 { Level: his king got out to g7, and he still has his queen. [%cal Gf3h3] }) (20. fxe5 { Taking the pawn gives him time, and his queen comes to g6. } 20... Qg6 21. Qh3+ Kg7 { About level: his queen came to g6 first, and your attack stalled. [%cal Gf3h3] }) (20. Rg3 { The rook goes to g3 instead, and his queen comes to g6 to meet it. } 20... Qg6 21. Qd7 Qe6 { Level: his queen blocked the g column, and the checks are over. [%cal Gf3h3] }) (20. f5 { The pawn push gives him time, and his queen blocks on h6. } 20... Qh6 21. Rh3 Bf6 { Level: his queen and bishop guard the h column, and the checks are over. [%cal Gf3h3] }) (20. Qh5+ { Check from h5, but his queen blocks on h6, and your rook is not ready. } 20... Qh6 21. Qf5+ Kg7 { Better for him: his king slipped out, and your attack is two pieces short. [%cal Gf3h3] }) (20. Qh3+ { Check from h3, and his queen blocks on h6. } 20... Qh6 21. Qd7 Bc8 { Better for him: your queen went wandering, and his queen stays safe on h6. [%cal Gf3h3] }) 20... Qh6 { His queen blocks on h6. } 21. Rxh6+ { The rook takes his queen with check. } (21. fxe5 { Taking the e pawn first gives his bishop time to leave b7. } 21... Bc8 22. Rxh6+ Kxh6 { Level: you won his queen for your rook, but his bishops are safe now and his rooks are coming. [%cal Gh3h6] }) (21. Qf5+ { Check from f5 first: the same trade in the other order, just as strong. } 21... Kg7 22. Rxh6 Kxh6 { Much better for you: the same queen for rook, a move later. [%cal Gh3h6] }) (21. Rf1 { The rook comes to f1 first, and the queen for rook trade still follows. } 21... f5 22. Rxh6+ Kxh6 { Much better for you, but the direct capture was simpler. [%cal Gh3h6] }) (21. Qd7 { The queen forks his bishops at once, but his queen is still on the board. } 21... Bh4 22. Qxb7 exf4 { Better for you, but his queen survived, and your attack slowed down. [%cal Gh3h6] }) 21... Kxh6 { His king takes back. } 22. Qd7 { The queen forks his two bishops, on b7 and e7. [%cal Gd7b7,Gd7e7] } (22. Qf5 { The queen goes to f5, and he guards his bishop on e7 with a rook. } 22... Rae8 23. Rf1 d4 24. e4 { Better for you, but the fork never came, and he kept both bishops. [%cal Gg4d7] }) (22. Qh3+ { A check first: good, but his king only goes back to g7. } 22... Kg7 23. Qg4+ Kh6 { Much better for you, but you are back where you started: the fork is still there to find. [%cal Gg4d7] }) (22. Rf1 { The rook comes to f1, and he pushes his f pawn to meet it. } 22... f5 23. Qh3+ Kg6 24. Qg3+ { Much better for you, but the bishops are both still his. [%cal Gg4d7] }) (22. fxe5 { Taking the pawn gives his bishop time to get out of the fork. } 22... Bc8 23. Qa4 b5 { Level: his bishops are safe, and his rooks are coming. [%cal Gg4d7] }) (22. d4 { The d pawn joins, but his f pawn moves forward and his bishops stay safe. } 22... f5 23. Qh3+ Kg7 24. fxe5 { Better for you, but the fork is gone: he covered his bishops in time. [%cal Gg4d7] }) 22... Rae8 { He guards the bishop on e7 with his rook, and the one on b7 is left hanging. } 23. Qxb7 { Queen and rook against two rooks and a bishop: you are ahead. Your bishops struck from both colours, on h7 and g7, and tore his king's cover open. That is what two bishops can do together. [%csl Gb7] } (23. Qh3+ { A check first, but his king just steps back, and the bishop on b7 gets time. } 23... Kg7 24. Qg4+ Kh6 { Better for you, but you are only checking, and the bishop on b7 is still his. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (23. Rf1 { The rook comes to f1, and his bishop gets time to step back from the fork. } 23... Bc8 24. Qc6+ Kg7 { Better for you, but his bishop ran to c8, where his rook on e8 guards it. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (23. Qxe7 { Taking the other bishop runs into his rook. } 23... Rxe7 { Lost for you: your queen for a bishop. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (23. Qxe8 { The queen takes the rook, but his other rook takes back. } 23... Rxe8 24. fxe5 Bc8 { Much better for him: you gave the queen for a rook. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (23. d4 { The d pawn joins, but his bishop on b7 escapes to c8. } 23... Bc8 24. Qc6+ Kg7 25. dxe5 { Better for you, but the fork is gone, and his bishop got away. [%cal Gd7b7] }) (23. fxe5 { Taking the pawn lets his bishop escape. } 23... Bc8 24. Qxd5 Rd8 { Slightly better for you, but the bishop got away. [%cal Gd7b7] }) 1-0
